

<% content_for :title, "Hinduism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .hinduism-bipolar-page .hinduism-copy,
  .hinduism-bipolar-page .hinduism-gunas > div,
  .hinduism-bipolar-page .hinduism-practice-list > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .hinduism-bipolar-page .hinduism-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>

<main class="hinduism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hinduism-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="hinduism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="hinduism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>HINDU LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="hinduism-bipolar-intro">
        YOUR MIND CHANGES.<br>
        YOU ARE MORE THAN ITS CURRENT STATE.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE SELF -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-pink" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-1-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        01 / THE SELF
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-1-title">
        YOU ARE<br>
        NOT JUST<br>
        YOUR MIND.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>“Established in yoga, perform actions, abandoning attachment, and remaining even minded in success and failure. Equanimity is called yoga.”</p>
        <cite class="hinduism-source">—Bhagavad Gita, 2.48</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        A chaplain recommended the Bhagavad Gita, and I took it back to my bunk.
        Following Arjuna and Krishna opened another way for me to understand bipolar
        disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        In several Hindu traditions, Atman names the deeper Self beyond changing
        thoughts, emotions, and mental states. Through this lens, your worth endures
        through mania, depression, or psychosis. You can recognize symptoms and own your
        actions without letting an episode define your identity.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / GUNAS -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-yellow" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-2-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        02 / THE GUNAS
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-2-title">
        THE MIND<br>
        HAS MODES.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        The three gunas describe changing qualities of nature and mind:
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-gunas">

        <div>
          <span>SATTVA</span>
          <p>Clarity, harmony, balance, and understanding.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>RAJAS</span>
          <p>Activity, desire, restlessness, and agitation.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>TAMAS</span>
          <p>Heaviness, inertia, obscurity, and reduced activity.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        They offer language for reflection, not medical explanations of mania or
        depression. Notice these qualities without assigning blame. Difficult symptoms
        do not mean spiritual failure.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / WITNESS -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-black" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-3-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        03 / THE WITNESS
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-3-title">
        WATCH<br>
        THE MIND.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        Witness consciousness means observing thoughts, feelings, and urges before
        identifying with or acting on them. “My mind feels urgent” leaves room for a
        response.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Katha Upanishad pictures the body as a chariot, the Self as its passenger,
        intellect as driver, mind as reins, and senses as horses. Knowing when symptoms
        make judgment unreliable—and accepting guidance—is part of discernment.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / DHARMA -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-purple" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-4-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        04 / DHARMA
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-4-title">
        WHAT IS<br>
        YOUR JOB<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        Karma concerns action and consequences; dharma concerns ethical duties and
        responsibilities. Bipolar disorder is not bad karma. Illness can impair
        judgment, and harm may still need repair.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your next responsibility might be treatment, honesty, rest, boundaries, or
        asking for help. Work with the choices available now, balancing accountability
        with compassion for how illness affects control.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / ATTACHMENT -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-pink" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-5-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        05 / ACTION & OUTCOME
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-5-title">
        DO THE WORK.<br>
        RELEASE THE<br>
        OUTCOME.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        Attachment to pleasure and resistance to pain can add struggle to an already
        difficult experience. Nonattachment means participating fully while loosening
        your demand for a particular outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        Act according to your values, continue care, and recognize what you cannot
        control. You can care deeply about recovery without demanding perfect stability.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / BALANCE -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-yellow" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-6-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        06 / BALANCE
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-6-title">
        BORING CAN<br>
        BE FUCKING<br>
        BEAUTIFUL.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        Equanimity, or samatva, means cultivating steadiness in how you respond to
        changing experiences. It leaves room for happiness, sadness, and difficult
        emotions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Acceptance means acknowledging your condition and limitations while continuing
        to seek help and make meaningful choices. Patience, routines, and self-
        compassion can be part of that practice.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="hinduism-section hinduism-black" aria-labelledby="hinduism-section-7-title">

    <div class="hinduism-section-heading">

      <p class="hinduism-number">
        07 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2 id="hinduism-section-7-title">
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-copy">

      <p>
        Yoga includes several paths beyond physical postures:
      </p>

      <div class="hinduism-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>KARMA YOGA / ACTION</strong><br>
            Meaningful action without attachment to outcomes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>JNANA YOGA / KNOWLEDGE</strong><br>
            Wisdom and self-inquiry beyond symptoms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>BHAKTI YOGA / DEVOTION</strong><br>
            Faith, connection, and self-compassion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>RAJA YOGA / MEDITATION</strong><br>
            Awareness and mental discipline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>HATHA YOGA / PHYSICAL DISCIPLINE</strong><br>
            Appropriate movement, breathing, and relaxation.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Spiritual practice can accompany psychiatric treatment. Choose practices with
        your condition and limitations in mind, and accept support when you need it.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="hinduism-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="hinduism-ending-small">
        THE MIND CHANGES.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU ARE<br>
        <span>STILL HERE.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="hinduism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Your dignity remains through changing mental states. Learn from the past, make
        amends, and allow yourself the possibility of renewal. You can find meaning and
        inner peace without having to be grateful for bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "hinduism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>


